# This machine

Host-specific facts that back the rules in `global-prompt.md`, kept out of the
public templates repo. The master copy is `~/.claude/global-machine.md` — edit
there and rerun `sync-global-prompt.ps1`.

> **Placeholders below.** Replace every `<...>` with this machine's real values.
> This file is always loaded, so keep it to **facts an agent cannot discover on
> its own**. Anything that is guidance rather than a fact belongs in
> `global-prompt.md`; anything discoverable (installed versions, running ports)
> belongs nowhere — the agent should just look.

## Machines on this LAN

| Machine | LAN IP | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `<DEV-BOX-NAME>` | `<10.0.0.x>` | where the code and dev servers live |
| `<DESK-BOX-NAME>` | `<10.0.0.y>` | the box I sit at, RDP'd into the dev box |

Because I RDP from one machine into another, the browser you're connected to is
frequently **not** on the machine running your shell and dev server — that's step
2 of the loopback ladder in `global-prompt.md`. Reach a dev server at
`http://<DEV-BOX-IP>:PORT`.

## Cloud accounts and project ids

- `<work-email>` is a **Workspace** account, so Gemini CLI needs
  `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=<gcp-project-id>` — normally supplied by `~/.gemini/.env`.

Gemini CLI does **not** merge env files; first match wins, walking up from cwd and
checking `.gemini/.env` then `.env` in each directory. So any repo that commits its
own root `.env` shadows `~/.gemini/.env`, and `gemini -p` dies with
`ProjectIdRequiredError` from inside that repo while working fine from `~`. That
silently drops cross-AI review to Codex only, since review runs from the repo root.

Fix per affected repo: create `.gemini/.env` at the repo root containing only
`GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=<gcp-project-id>` — it wins as step 1 in the chain, and the
app's own dotenv loader reads `.env`, so app GCP calls are unaffected. Gitignore
just that file, not the whole `.gemini/` directory. Verify from the repo root:
`gemini -p "Reply with one word: PONG"`.

## Shell

`<OS and version>`. `<primary shell>` is the primary shell; `<secondary shell>` is
available for the other syntax. Match the syntax to the shell you're invoking —
don't send PowerShell syntax to a POSIX shell or the reverse.

## Where the repos live

Clones live under `<~/Code>`. The `templates` repo — the source for
`global-prompt.md`, `gsd-settings.md`, and `ai-setup-audit.md` — is at
`<~/Code/templates>`; re-run its `sync-global-prompt.ps1` after editing any of
them.
